<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Central Powers</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Central Powers were the coalition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> that fought against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Their defeat in 1918 led to the collapse of all four monarchies. For the Ottoman Empire, the alliance led to the Allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/occupation-of-istanbul">occupation of Istanbul</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sevres">Treaty of Sèvres</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/central-powers.jpg" alt="Central Powers" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Publ. Photochemie Berlin No. 3603 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leaders_of_the_Central_Powers_-_Vierbund.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Central Powers, also called the Central Empires or the Quadruple Alliance, were one of the two main coalitions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. The name comes from the position of the members, all four lying between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in the east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the United Kingdom in the west. The alliance began with the pact between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> of 1879. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> had nominally joined that bloc in the Triple Alliance but did not fight on its side and later joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>. Germany also planned a Mitteleuropa economic association with Austria-Hungary and others.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> on 28 July 1914. Germany declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and, on 3 August, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, bound to Germany by a secret alliance signed on 2 August 1914, stayed formally neutral for nearly two months. It entered the war on 29 October 1914 after a raid on Russian ports by cruisers that had been transferred from Germany. Bulgaria, defeated by Serbia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> in July 1913, signed a treaty of defensive alliance with the Ottomans on 19 August 1914 and in October 1915 declared war on Serbia.</p><p>Other groups cooperated with the coalition. The Senussi Order, led by Ahmed Sharif as-Senussi, attacked the Italians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and the British in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Sultanate of Darfur</a> sided with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1915, and Irish nationalists launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-rising">Easter Rising</a> in 1916. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> lost its colonies to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a>, resisted until November 1918 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-lettow-vorbeck">Paul von Lettow-Vorbeck</a>, was the only one not fully conquered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>As the war lengthened, solidarity weakened. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a>, his successor Charles sought to distance <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and from 1916 its leaders wanted to leave the conflict. In September 1918 a Franco-Serbian offensive under Louis Franchet d'Espèrey broke the Bulgarian front, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> signed an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> on 29 September 1918. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> followed on 30 October, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> made ceasefires in the first week of November after the disintegration of the Habsburg Empire, and Germany signed on 11 November 1918. No single treaty ended the war: each of the Central Powers was dealt with in a separate treaty. The alliance dissolved in 1918.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Central Powers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Russian rule effectively ended with the Great Retreat of 1915, when the <b>Central Powers</b> occupied the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1916 it declared war on the <b>Central Powers</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-romania">Kingdom of Romania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its alignment with the <b>Central Powers</b> in the First World War led to the Allied occupation of Istanbul from 1918 and the punitive Treaty of Sèvres in 1920.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In March 1918 the government moved the capital from Petrograd to Moscow and signed the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk with the <b>Central Powers</b>, giving up Poland, the Baltic lands, Finland and Ukraine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In July 1918 the Communist Party (Bolsheviks) of Ukraine was founded in Moscow, and after the <b>Central Powers</b>' defeat a Provisional Workers' and Peasants' Government was set up at Kursk in November 1918.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ukrainian-ssr">Ukrainian SSR · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It was meant to rally American men to enlist in the American Expeditionary Forces and fight the <b>Central Powers</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/over-there">Over There</a></li></ul></section>
